# Vitamin Helix — Projects/Detail/Briefing Redesign: Architecture Decisions

**Status:** Living document. Captures decisions made for work still ahead. The Projects page's core functionality (filtering, sort, column resize/reorder, row expansion) is live — its architecture, conventions, and current state live in `Projects.md`, not here; this doc no longer carries Projects-page wireframe/build history, only what still applies to it going forward plus everything for the Detail and Briefing pages, which haven't been rebuilt yet.
**Last updated:** 29 Jul 2026 (corrected — a prior version of this doc and of `Projects.md` described a box-per-group table redesign and a working `ProjectTableView` saved-views system as already built; neither is actually present in the live codebase, confirmed by reading the real files directly this session. See `Projects.md`'s own correction note for details.)

---

## Ground rules for this whole initiative

1. **Scope of "rewrite from scratch":** templates, CSS, JS, and route *rendering* logic for the Projects page(s), Detail page, and Briefing/Create page. NOT the DB schema (unchanged, no data loss on deploy) and NOT most of the proven backend business logic (query functions, the status-log funnel, notification triggers, achievement hooks) — that gets reused/refactored-in-place, not re-derived from memory, unless a specific piece is confirmed broken below.
2. **Benchmark:** the Dashboard (`dashboard.html`/`dashboard_cs.html`/`dashboard_leadership.html`/`dashboard_designer.html` + `dashboard.css`/`dashboard.js`) is the visual and code-quality bar. It is NOT being redesigned — one small, additive exception below (Escalation History).
3. **Sequencing:** Projects page first (core functionality live — see `Projects.md`; Group-by and Saved Views from an earlier plan were never actually built), then Briefing/Create, then Detail page last (largest/riskiest — 205KB template, ~73 routes across 5 files).
4. **Branch:** work happens on `dev`. `main` stays clean/deployable for the full 2 weeks in case a production hotfix is needed. Ezekiel commits current WIP to `main`, pushes, then switches to `dev`.
5. **Working mode:** Ezekiel writes all the code himself this initiative (rebuilding manual-coding muscle memory). Claude gives code + explanation, one chunk at a time, never applies it directly — except where Ezekiel explicitly grants a one-time exception (e.g. the Projects page scroll-bug fix + Sort feature, 29 Jul).
6. **Future-migration principle (added 17 Jul 2026):** Ezekiel plans to eventually move Helix to a proper Flutter application once HR/production/accounting modules are also built. For THIS rewrite: write clean, modular code with business logic decoupled from templates/routes (already the plan) so a future migration is easier — but do NOT build a separate JSON API layer yet. That's explicitly deferred until the app's shape stabilizes across more modules; adding it now would mean maintaining two surfaces in parallel while things are still changing fast.
7. **Client-side performance principle (added 21 Jul 2026)** — see "Client-Side Performance" section below. Server response time is not the bottleneck anywhere in this app; client-side rendering/DOM cost is. The Projects page was built with this in mind from day one (see `Projects.md`); Detail and Briefing get the same treatment when built. The Dashboard itself gets a dedicated optimization pass afterward.

---

## Client-Side Performance (locked 21 Jul 2026)

Raised because the Dashboard — the app's own code-quality benchmark — is noticeably sluggish on the client side today, despite the server being fast. Diagnosed directly against the real `dashboard.js`/`dashboard.css` (not guessed) before deciding what to do differently in the new pages.

**Root causes found in the Dashboard, for reference:**
- **Every card's full body renders into the DOM on every page load, for every tab, regardless of which one is active.** `.dash-card-body-content` (the wrapper every card's content sits in) defaults to `max-height: 0; overflow: hidden;` and only becomes visible via a `.expanded` class — the collapsed tabs' rows, avatars, and chip markup are all still fully built and sitting in the DOM, just visually clipped.
- **`initCards()` forces a synchronous layout read on load.** It walks every already-`.expanded` body and reads `body.scrollHeight` inside a `forEach` — `scrollHeight` can't be answered without the browser fully laying out that subtree first, so this forces real layout work at exactly the moment the page is trying to become interactive.
- **`dashboard.js` is one 162KB file** handling the CS, leadership, and designer dashboard variants all at once — every role's browser parses and compiles code paths it will never use.
- **Avatar images aren't lazy-loaded** — `dash_person_chip()`'s `<img>` tag has no `loading="lazy"`, so every avatar image on the page (including ones inside collapsed, invisible cards) gets requested immediately.

**Principle for the remaining new pages, going forward:** render-on-demand, not render-everything-and-hide.
- **Don't pre-render collapsed/inactive states into the DOM.** The Detail page's rail sub-sections (Design/Finance/Production/Logistics, and within Design: Project Details/Deliverables/Submissions) should build/inject their markup at the moment they're actually shown (fetch-on-click, or clone from a `<template>` element), not sit fully built in the DOM from first paint just waiting for a CSS class to reveal them.
- **Never force a layout read (`scrollHeight`/`offsetHeight`/`getBoundingClientRect`) inside a loop right after a DOM write.** Read measurements in one pass, then write style/DOM changes in a separate pass, if a measurement is ever genuinely needed.
- **Event delegation, not one listener per row.** Deliverables sections (per the C&CM example, 90+ deliverables) should attach one listener on a shared container and read `event.target`, not attach a listener to every row/chip individually — same pattern already proven on the Projects page.
- **JS stays split per page/department** — `detail-design.js`/`detail-finance.js`/etc. are separate files, so a given page only ever loads the JS it actually needs.
- **Debounce live-filtering/search inputs** so a full re-render doesn't fire per keystroke.
- **Lazy-load avatar images** (`loading="lazy"` on every `<img>`) — already applied in `person_chip()`.

**Dashboard optimization pass — explicitly deferred, not part of this rewrite's 3-page scope.**

