## Detail Page — Tab Framework (locked 17 Jul 2026, still provisional pending Ezekiel's new idea — see note below)

The Detail page is being restructured into department tabs, built now as a framework even though most departments don't have real content yet.

**Tabs:** Design | Finance | Production | Logistics.
- **Design tab** = today's entire existing detail-page content, unchanged access (CS, Designer, Team Lead, Admin, Management keep exactly the access they have today).
- **Finance / Production / Logistics tabs** = new, built incrementally. Visible-but-disabled for everyone except admin/management until each has real content.

**Final role-gating matrix** (applies once a given tab actually has content):
- Designer / Team Lead → Design + Production
- Finance → all four tabs
- CS → all four tabs
- Management → all four tabs
- Logistics → Logistics only
- Production → Design + Production + Logistics

**Sidebar architecture (locked 17 Jul 2026):** the app's global sidebar stays exactly as-is. The Detail page's department-tab navigation becomes a SECOND, separate rail sliding out attached immediately to the right of the global sidebar — Design/Finance/Production/Logistics, then Project Files + Flag Issue at lighter weight, a divider, then Put Project on Hold + Delete Project, then Back. Visually distinguished with a slightly different background shade.

**Inline editing everywhere — supersedes "Project Settings" and any separate "Edit Brief" flow (locked 17 Jul 2026).** Every section of the Detail page becomes directly editable in place. The "Project Settings" floating menu is removed entirely — "Edit Project" is gone, the remaining two actions (Put Project on Hold, Delete Project) become direct rail buttons.
- **Edit Deliverables button:** restricted to CS/Admin/Management, renders the same edit-mode UI as the Briefing page's own Deliverables edit mode — one shared component.
- **Everything is editable, including generated identifiers.** Job Number and FOC Number are inline-editable too — build the necessary safeguards (concurrency/uniqueness checks) rather than lock these down. **Flag for whoever builds this:** both identifiers likely get referenced elsewhere (file/deck naming, folder paths) — check those downstream references don't silently go stale when one is edited after the fact.
- **Roles confirmed: CS/Admin/Management**, uniform gate across all newly-inline-editable brief content.
- **Save mechanism:** each inline-editable div gets its own explicit "Save Changes" button (not silent autosave), and on save, the change updates instantly for everyone else currently viewing that project via the app's existing SSE infrastructure. **Concurrent-edit conflict:** a warning, not a silent overwrite.

**Rail sub-sections — generic mechanism (locked 17 Jul 2026):** whichever department tab is active, its own sub-sections appear as an indented list directly beneath it — the rail grows TALLER, not wider. Design is the first to use this (Project Details / Deliverables / Submissions), but the mechanism is generic for future departments.

**Rail height — accordion behavior.** Only one department's sub-sections are ever expanded at a time.

**"Project Files" rail item:** a direct shortcut to the project's root NAS folder — distinct from the in-app "Reference Files" card (uploaded reference docs inside Project Details) and each deliverable's own Files Button (NAS shortcut, deliverable level).

**Dependency flagged, not blocking:** Finance/Production/Logistics aren't valid `User.role` values today. Adding them is small and additive but touches the User model/auth layer — fold it in when the actual gating logic gets built.

**Note — still provisional as of 28 Jul 2026:** Ezekiel has a new idea for the Detail page's redesign. Everything in this section and the two sections below it (Design Tab Content, Deliverables C&CM) reflects the wireframe pass locked 17 Jul 2026 — treat it as the prior baseline, not final, until the new idea has been walked through and this doc is updated to reflect it. **This is the single biggest piece of work still ahead.**

