## Code Architecture & Data-Model Migration Plan

Translates the decisions above into concrete file/route architecture and a catalog of data-model changes. Verified 20 Jul 2026 directly against the real `app/models/__init__.py` and `app/status_tracking.py` — most "new tables" originally assumed turned out to already exist; the real gaps are smaller and more specific.

### A. Tables to DROP entirely

| Table | Why |
|---|---|
| `ProjectApproval` (`project_approvals`) | Confirmed dead — 0 rows, nothing reads/writes it. |
| `ProjectReviewer` (`project_reviewers`) | Almost certainly dead — one lingering row, drop only after eyeballing `SELECT * FROM project_reviewers` to confirm it's inert. Not blocking. |

### B. Columns to DROP

| Column | Why |
|---|---|
| `Deliverable.brief_flag` | Verified via grep — unread/unwritten. Superseded by the real `BriefFlag`/`BriefFlagMessage` system, which is NOT being touched by this rewrite. |
| `Deliverable.brief_flag_resolved` | Same as above. |
| `Project.hours_accumulated`, `Project.timer_started_at` | `status_tracking.py`'s own docstring confirms these are no longer written to by anything — hours are fully recomputed on demand from `ProjectStatusLog` history. |
| `Project.status` | **Decided 20 Jul 2026: drop it.** `record_project_status()` — the single funnel for every project status change — only ever writes `project.project_status`, never `project.status`. Pull it and fix forward if anything breaks. |

### C. Fields whose MEANING changes

- **`Project.project_status`** is the real, live flat status field. For Standard briefs, becomes purely the coarse blanket status. For C&CM, `ProjectCustomer.status` already exists and already IS the per-customer blanket status — no new column needed.
- **Decided 20 Jul 2026: collapse `ProjectSubmission`'s flag-combination into one explicit `workflow_status` column** — additive, not destructive. Backfill plan from existing signals: `submitted_to_client_at IS NULL` → `'draft'`; set + `is_flagged=True` → `'revision_requested'`; set + not flagged + parent approved → `'approved'`; set + not flagged + parent not approved → `'sent_to_client'`. All existing fields (`is_active`, `is_flagged`, `flag_message`, `flagged_by_id`, `flagged_at`, `submitted_to_client_at`, `submitted_by_id`) stay exactly as-is — new code reads/writes `workflow_status` as the source of truth but keeps setting the old fields alongside it. Actually removing the redundant flags is deferred to a later cleanup pass.
- **Open decision, unchanged:** clean cutover vs. backfill for in-flight projects' `project_status` the day this ships.

### D. New tables needed

1. **`ProjectTableView`** — **not actually built.** An earlier version of this doc and of `Projects.md` claimed this was built and confirmed working on 29 Jul 2026; that was inaccurate — verified this session by reading the live `project_list.py` directly, which has no `ProjectTableView` usage at all. Still needed if Saved Views gets picked up as real work.
2. **A "has this user seen this project's Detail page before" marker — still needed.** `SidebarClick` only tracks navigating back to a previous page, not page views generally. Build a small dedicated table: `id, user_id, project_id, first_viewed_at`.

Everything else originally guessed at as new — Submission tracking, per-deliverable/per-customer revision counters, POSM channels, install date — **already exists**: `ProjectSubmission`, `ProjectSubmissionDeliverable`, `ProjectSubmissionFile`, `TechnicalSubmission`, `ProjectRevision`/`ProjectRevisionDeliverable`, `ProjectPosmChannel`, `ProjectCustomer.installation_date`, `Project.installation_date`, `Project.concept_options_required`/`kv_options_required`, `ProjectCustomer.posm_revision_count`, `Project.revision_count`.

### E. New columns actually needed

- `Project.is_deleted`, `Project.deleted_at`, `Project.deleted_by_id` — soft-delete.
- Atomic job-number mechanism — Postgres `SEQUENCE` or a counter table claimed via `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`.
- `ProjectSubmission.last_internal_review_notified_at` — needed for the modified-since-notification check.
- `ProjectSubmission.cs_note` (nullable) — optional note at Sent-to-Client/Approved.
- `User.role` whitelist gaining `Finance`/`Production`/`Logistics`.

### F. Gulf per-customer submission routes — likely a logic change, not a schema change

**Confirmed 20 Jul 2026: Gulf customers are already `ProjectCustomer` rows**, same mechanism as UAE. `Customer.region` already tags every customer's country; `ProjectPosmChannel.posm_customer_id` is already nullable and ready to use. The fix is changing the channel-creation logic (in `projects_submission.py`/`projects_approval.py`) to always set `posm_customer_id` for Gulf too.

### G. Undo-Approved and file-cleanup — simpler than first assumed

- **No new `previous_status` snapshot column needed.** `ProjectStatusLog`/`ProjectCustomerStatusLog`/`DeliverableStatusLog` already bracket every status period with `started_at`/`ended_at` — the row before "approved" already IS the prior state.
- **Cleanup of auto-copied DWG/PDF files on undo probably doesn't need a new `copied_to_path` column** — the auto-copy code already has to construct that destination path, so cleanup can likely just re-derive it. Worth confirming before assuming a new column is needed.

### H. Code/template/route removals still ahead

- Today's monolithic `detail.js` — replaced by one JS+CSS pair per department tab.
- The "Project Settings" floating menu — replaced by two direct rail buttons.
- Any separate "Edit Brief"/"Edit Project" page or route — superseded by inline editing.
- Dead "Remove secondary CS" confirmation modal + its JS wiring.
- `flagRevision()`'s dead `onclick`-attribute selector line.
- `work_hours_between()`.
- The legacy no-channel-object Gulf POSM fallback code path.
- Native `confirm()` calls — unify on `showConfirm()`.
- The old Review & Submit modal's stale copy.

### I. File/route architecture still ahead

- **Detail page:** one JS+CSS pair per department tab — `detail-design.*` first, consolidating today's Design-tab logic (currently spread across `projects_detail.py`/`projects_brief.py`/`projects_approval.py`/`projects_submission.py`).
- **Briefing page:** single JS/CSS pair, refactoring `projects_brief.py`.
- **SSE reuse:** inline-editing saves and the modified-since-notification check hook into whatever function already emits SSE events — likely just 1-2 new event types.

